hello everyone this is sean taylor field  application scientist manager for gen  script in north america  and in this talk we'll be exploring  the key questions around  the testing strategies implemented in a  sars cove ii post post-vaccination era  with mixed populations of vaccinated and  non-vaccinated individuals  so three main questions arise  surrounding the current dynamic in the  sars cove2 pandemic and the first one is  associated with just the vaccines  themselves  they all have widely varying efficacies  so how is protection assured  post-vaccination in the context of both  the circulating wild type and also all  the variance strains  the second question relates to immunity  and how long it persists  post-vaccination this is a big question  that's remains unanswered currently  and then the third question is what is  the most actionable information in  populations of mixed vaccinated and  non-vaccinated individuals  that we can use from testing to help  assure that we control this pandemic  going forward  so all of the available tests for sars  co2 infection fall under two major  categories  serology  and molecular  in the early days of the pandemic before  we had vaccination the primary mode of  testing was molecular testing which was  using pcr  and that's because  pcr is very sensitive in being able to  measure the presence of the virus and if  we look at this graph here where we look  at onset onset of symptoms versus time  you can see that the viral rna is the  first thing that we can measure and  that's using a pcr test or a molecular  test and that's to get early detection  of infection so that people can isolate  and quarantine themselves to prevent  spread of infection and that's what we  were doing initially  but now that we have vaccination  vaccination elicits an immune response  which generates antibodies and those  antibodies can be measured to assess  how effective the vaccine is  and those antibodies come a little later  in time they're usually only measurable  about seven days seven days to two weeks  post-vaccination or post-infection if  you're naturally infected with cyrus  cove too  however these antibodies persist they  they typically persist for several  months and in some cases even years  so that they continue to offer  protection or immunity against disease  for long periods of time and this has  been shown in many different viruses not  yet in sars cove too but definitely  the model is there in multiple viruses  so  within the antibodies that are elicited  for infection are antibodies that are  termed neutralizing antibodies i'm going  to call them nabs  and these neutralizing antibodies  very specifically bind to the virus in  such a way  that they block the virus from binding  to the host cell ace2 receptors so this  is this is the the mechanism of action  of the virus it binds very strongly to  the h2 receptor of cells and that  permits the virus then to inject its  viral rna into the cell to replicate  itself and that's what propagates  infection  so these specific nabs  block the virus from binding there are  lots of antibodies generated but only a  subset of them which are neutralizing  essentially neutralize the virus prevent  it from binding to the h2 receptor and  therefore from replicating in the cells  so these are the antibodies that are  really important in an immune response  dr lin fauwang  invented a test that uses the  proteins that are associated with  the docking of the virus to the cell  and by purifying just the tip of the  spike protein which is the receptor  binding domain here in brown  and the extracellular domain of the ace2  receptor these are the two interacting  subunits of proteins that actually  cause the virus to dock to the cell so  both the virus and the live cells no  longer are required in this test we're  just using the protein components that  interact with each other  so in this test the elisa plates are  coated with the ace2 receptor as we see  here in green  and the rbd is in solution and by mixing  the receptor binding domain  with the patient sample  the antibodies from the patient sample  will bind to the rbd  and those antibodies that are  neutralizing the antibodies in red here  will block the rbd from binding to the  ac2 receptor non-neutralizing antibodies  can still bind but they're not blocking  in the case where the individual doesn't  have neutralizing antibodies only these  blue non-neutralizing antibodies then  the rbd can bind to the ace2 receptor  and this binding event is very very  strong and  then we will elicit a color change in  the solution because these rbd molecules  are are conjugated to horseradish  peroxidase which elicits a color change  when mixed with tmb  so there are a few major advantages of  the c-pass neutralizing antibody test  and the first one is that it's  immunoglobulin agnostic  what this means is that any class of  immunoglobulins iggs igms igas that  elicit neutralizing antibodies as  depicted here in red can bind to the rbd  there is no  secondary antibody that forces the  measure of a particular class of  immunoglobulins and that increases the  sensitivity of this assay versus other  more traditional elisa tests that are  just measuring binding antibodies  the second advantage is that the rbd is  in solution it's not coated on the  surface of the plate by having the rbd  in solution it's in a near native  conformation and therefore the antigenic  sites  that are presented to the antibodies in  the rbd  force the antibodies to bind to the more  native antigenic sites very specifically  so this increases the specificity of the  assay versus  coated plates that have rbd coated on  the surface of the plate which may  perturb the confirmation of the rbd or  spike protein or nucleocapsid protein  and and then elicit antigenic sites that  may be non-native and may and then other  immunoglobulins may bind causing  non-specific binding so this assay is  highly specific  and finally the last advantage which is  the most important advantage is that  it's a dual detection and functional  assay remember this assay is measuring  the binding event of rbd  to ace2 receptor  that's being perturbed by neutralizing  antibodies so we do have some data that  we  generated in a recent article that we  published in  the journal of clinical microbiology and  there are a number of other papers that  have been published that corroborate  these kind of results  what we did was we looked at  68 samples 45 presumed positive with pcr  and 23  negative samples that were pre-pandemic  and we tested them with three different  commercial assays that use coated plates  either rbd coated plates spike s1 coated  plates or nucleocapsid coated plates we  compared them with the rc pass svnt test  for neutralizing antibodies if we look  first on the right side of each of the  graphs at the  presumed negative samples these were the  pre-pandemic samples we see that the rbd  coated plate assay gave three false  positives  also the nucleocapsid coated plate gave  false positives and this is this is not  uncommon for these types of commercial  tests with these types of coated protein  plates  the only assay that didn't give false  positives  was the spike s1 coated plate but we  have seen other commercial assays with  spike s1 coated plates that do give  false positives and all this is  associated  probably with the fact that by coating  the plate with these proteins as i  mentioned in the previous slide the  protein itself may be perturbed and bind  other immunoglobulins therefore that are  unrelated to sars cove too  the c-pass test gave no false positives  as expected  if we go to the  the positive samples we see with the rbd  coated plate that it called the majority  of the samples as positive 43 out of 45  only two false negatives but again it  called several negative samples false  falsely positive so it's difficult to  assess the validity of these results  if we look at the spike s1 coded plate  it only called 34 of the 45 samples  positive 11 were called falsely negative  and and the nucleocapsid plate was a  little worse with 28 called positive 17  called falsely negative c-pass did very  well with 39 positives and six negatives  and when we looked at the false negative  samples there were six we just wanted to  know if these six samples were also  called negative in at least the  nucleocapsid and spike s1 coated plates  and they were  which  leads us to the potential conclusion  that these six samples were in fact true  negative samples and may be called  falsely positive by qpcr which has been  shown  to happen  with qpcr especially with tests that  have very high cq value cutoffs  in another study in the same article we  looked at the comparative data between  the c-pass test the genscript c-pass  test with a number of very commonly used  igg binding assay tests  and what we found was if we look at  day's post-symptom onset we were able to  see that the c-pass test gave fairly  good results in the five to nine day  period there were a few tests that gave  a hundred percent but c-pass gave 86 so  still  very solid data in terms of sensitivity  uh for this assay even early on post  symptom onset  when we reached the 10 to 19 uh day  period though c pass excelled so it gave  92 sensitivity which was the best  sensitivity among all of the tests  and when we reached greater than 19 days  all the tests performed equally well  with 100 sensitivity  if we go and look at all the time points  combined c-pass was again at the top of  the heap with 93 sensitivity  and we also looked at some negative  samples and again some of the tests gave  some false positives c-pass called all  the samples negative as expected so 100  specificity  so very good data for the c-pass results  in terms of assessing  people who have been infected and  recovered from sars cove ii  of course the major question that people  would have  would be how does c-pass  results relate to the gold standard in  live virus tests because the gold  standard really is live virus testing  and if we look at um plaque reducing  neutralization test which is the most  commonly used live virus test c-pass for  66 samples gave excellent corroborative  data so  c-pass called 26 of the 66 samples  positive 40 negative and with prnt at a  higher stringency which is what's  recommended to be used with this type of  test either pr t 75 or 90 39 of the  samples were called negative and 27  positive so only one sample in red here  was called negative for c pass but  positive for prnt so very close  correlation a word of caution we did  compare c-pass with frnt which is  another live virus test foci reducing  neutralization test and what we found  quite surprisingly was that with frnt by  using different analysis stringencies  the data completely changes  so  whereas with a 50  stringency most of the samples were  called positive  at a 90 stringency only about half the  samples were called positive so it's it  just lends a word of caution that with  these live virus tests it's important to  really  assess and corroborate the analysis  stringency with the true  result  of the data because the data can change  a lot if you change the analysis  stringency whereas cpass was quite  consistent  another article that was published for  live virus assays so they looked at 401  human samples also dogs cats and  hamsters and and between all of those  samples the correlation of the c-pass  test the spnt assay with prnt90  was  very good 99  specificity and sensitivity uh  correlated with prnt9 and there are  other articles that have corroborated  these results as well so this is just  one of many  the c-pass test is  amenable to semi-quantitative analysis  we have produced a  ru a research use only protocol that can  be used to assess the tighter levels of  neutralizing antibodies and  that it all relates to just producing a  standard curve with a standard that we  provide where you can generate a  standard curve and then the unknown  samples are related to the standard  curve to get tighter values  by multiplying the tighter values by the  dilution factor used for the sample you  can get a final neutralizing antibody  titer here's some data that we looked at  with 25 samples where if you look at the  final neutralizing titer  across this set of samples  you have people that have very low  titers these were all people that were  infected and then recovered from sars  cove 2  not vaccinated but 148 nanograms per mil  and then in some people it went up to 90  000 so very wide range of neutralizing  antibody titers post infection and  recovery  so this begs the question  it's probably going to be very  individual basis  how long neutralizing antibodies persist  from person to person if we look at some  real world efficacy data for the  vaccines even after two doses the  vaccines range from 95 percent with  pfizer all the way down to 50 percent  with the cineback vaccines there's a  pretty wide range even after two doses  of efficacy of vaccine  with one dose of vaccine  the efficacies are can be even lower so  very important to assure that we remain  vigilant and cautious to isolate  ourselves and wear masks after one dose  of vaccine because the efficacy is not  that high after one dose  if we move to the variance strains we  can see how the variance strains impact  vaccine efficacy where where for the  astral vaccine it's over 70 percent uh  after two doses but it drops down to  only 10 percent in the presence of the  south african strain that's why south  africa is no longer using this vaccine  uh for um their programs  if we look at the johnson johnson  vaccine um again it's 72 percent after  one dose and it drops down to 50 to 57  percent in the presence of the south  african variant so just  these are the  big questions that we're faced with how  do we deal with the variance and  vaccination and which vaccines are the  ones that are going to give us the most  protection and how do we measure all  this how do we keep track of whether or  not we're protected uh in this in this  wild west of varying vaccine efficacies  so  over the past year and a half  there's been a rapidly increasing body  of evidence that supports the  correlation between the presence of sars  co2 neutralizing antibodies and it an  inhibition of viral propagation this has  also already been shown for other  viruses so this is not a big surprise  the big concern is associated with  vaccines and the efficacy of vaccines  where we've already shown from the  previous slide that there's a marked  decrease in efficacy particularly with  the south african variant and in  particular with the astrazeneca vaccine  however there these prevailing data from  a variety of articles shows that  for many of the vaccines the  neutralizing antibodies the power of  those antibodies is still quite robust  in neutralizing the varying strains from  binding to host cells  so although there might be some vaccines  that are much less effective most  vaccines are more than adequate to  generate the level of neutralizing  antibodies that are required to prevent  the virus from binding to host cells and  propagating  so all of this leads to a general  conclusion therefore that the direct  measurement of neutralizing antibodies  that are elicited post-vaccination  could therefore be a strong indicator of  a productive  and a functional response to sars cove2  and this can be measured now in a simple  c-pass test  so what do we now know about sars cov2  testing  for molecular testing we know that a pcr  test represents just a moment in time  whereby a negative test only means that  a person is negative at the moment that  they took the test  it doesn't assure protection from future  stars co2 infection  we also know that the presence of  binding antibodies so total igg antibody  tests do not necessarily mean that  functional neutralizing antibodies are  circulating  in the context of neutralizing  antibodies we know that they can persist  for months and possibly longer but we  also know that this might vary between  individuals  and given that vaccine efficacy  especially in the context of the  variants can vary widely a c-pass test  neutralizing antibody test can help  assure that the expected productive and  functional response to vaccination was  elicited  also neutralizing antibodies can be  measured with c-pass longitudinally to  periodically assess titers over time to  follow neutralizing antibody levels  post-vaccination  so  what could be presented as a strategy  for a mixed vaccinated and unvaccinated  population  is a combined approach use pcr for those  that don't have immunity  and c-pass can be used for vaccinated  individuals  as an optimal approach to minimize the  risk of a future pandemic

